          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is osteoarthritis not simply “wear and tear” of a joint?",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is a problem of the whole joint, not just the cartilage. The \"Osteoarthritis\" reference summary describes it as a disorder affecting cartilage, the bone underneath, ligaments, the joint lining and the surrounding muscles. Cartilage is progressively lost, and the bone beneath it remodels in response.\n\nThe same summary says the process is active rather than simple mechanical erosion. It involves inflammation and attempted repair. It also notes that reduced use leads to muscle weakness, which makes the joint worse. This is why the summary treats exercise as first-line treatment and why calling the condition \"wear and tear\" can lead people to rest the joint when movement may help.\n\nThe passage does not explain the specific inflammatory or repair mechanisms in detail. A clinician can discuss how this applies to a particular joint.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
